 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is deterministic and based on point cloud queries via K-D trees [S1]. Complexity depends on the number of points sampled per ray and the efficiency of the K-D tree traversal [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Stores the point cloud, normals, and neural network parameters [S1]. More memory than standard NeRF due to explicit point storage, but less than mesh-based methods if the mesh is complex [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves state-of-the-art performance in shape deformation and scene morphing tasks [S1]. High-fidelity rendering results are produced [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Supports zero-shot inference, meaning editing does not require retraining, which is computationally efficient [S1]. Fast fine-tuning can be applied for higher quality [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Native shape editing via point manipulation; intrinsic integration of point clouds and NeRF; high-fidelity rendering; supports general scene editing operations difficult to achieve with mesh-guided deformation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial point cloud; requires careful tuning of the pruning and growing strategy; Phong reflection model assumes simple lighting conditions [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When native shape editing of NeRFs is required, especially for general deformation and morphing tasks [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When simple novel-view synthesis without editing is needed (standard NeRF is sufficient); when complex lighting environments require more advanced shading models than Phong [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering time depends on the number of samples per ray and the efficiency of the K-D tree traversal. The K-D tree allows for efficient neighbor search and voxel intersection, improving stability and potentially speed compared to uniform sampling [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the point cloud, K-D tree, and NeRF parameters. The K-D tree adds overhead but enables density-adaptive structures [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** NeuralEditor achieves state-of-the-art performance in shape deformation and scene morphing tasks [S1]. It supports zero-shot inference, which is computationally cheaper than retraining, and fast fine-tuning for enhanced quality [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Native shape editing via point manipulation; intrinsic integration of point clouds and NeRF; high-fidelity rendering with Phong shading; supports general scene editing operations difficult for mesh-based methods [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial point cloud; may require fine-tuning for perfect lighting consistency after editing [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When explicit shape editing of NeRFs is required, especially for general deformation and morphing tasks [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When high-quality texture synthesis without geometric editing is the primary goal, or when computational resources for K-D tree construction and maintenance are limited.
 
 ## Background
-NeuralEditor was proposed by Jun-Kun Chen, Jipeng Lyu, and Yu-Xiong Wang from the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign and Peking University [S1]. The paper was published in 2023 (arXiv:2305.03049v1, CVPR 2023) [S1]. The work addresses the fundamental challenge of editing the shape of scenes in NeRFs, which traditionally lack this capability [S1]. It builds on the insight that NeRF rendering can be interpreted as plotting a 3D point cloud to a 2D image plane [S1]. The method is used in applications such as visual simulation, movie, and game industries, where free-form editing of 3D objects and scenes is required [S1].
